1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,269
1 a.m., September 5th, 1950.

2
00:00:03,289 --> 00:00:05,992
A mountain valley north of Yeongcheon.

3
00:00:06,108 --> 00:00:07,772
In the pouring rain, five North

4
00:00:07,792 --> 00:00:10,486
Korean tanks began rolling south.

5
00:00:10,883 --> 00:00:14,051
Yeongcheon was a crossroads: the Taegu–Pohang

6
00:00:14,071 --> 00:00:16,974
road met the road south to Gyeongju.

7
00:00:17,013 --> 00:00:18,792
If it broke here, the waist of the

8
00:00:18,812 --> 00:00:21,579
Naktong line would be cut clean through.

9
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:24,000
Turn west, and the troops fighting at

10
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:26,538
Dabu-dong would be hit from behind.

11
00:00:26,636 --> 00:00:29,200
Push south to Gyeongju, and almost nothing

12
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:31,623
stood between the enemy and Pusan.

13
00:00:32,167 --> 00:00:34,802
Then, on September 6th, North Korean

14
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:37,431
troops entered the town of Yeongcheon.

15
00:00:37,509 --> 00:00:39,322
The waist of the Naktong line

16
00:00:39,342 --> 00:00:41,250
had finally been pierced.

17
00:00:41,638 --> 00:00:43,348
Yet one week later, the North

18
00:00:43,368 --> 00:00:45,329
Korean division that took Yeongcheon

19
00:00:45,349 --> 00:00:47,217
would vanish from the map.

20
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:49,171
This is the Battle of Yeongcheon,

21
00:00:49,191 --> 00:00:52,090
the hidden reversal that held the line.

22
00:00:52,485 --> 00:00:56,153
By August 1950, South Korean and UN forces

23
00:00:56,173 --> 00:00:58,805
had been pushed back to the Naktong River.

24
00:00:58,846 --> 00:01:01,614
From Masan through Waegwan to Yeongdeok,

25
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:04,577
it was their last line of defense.

26
00:01:04,972 --> 00:01:07,164
All through August, the North Koreans went

27
00:01:07,184 --> 00:01:09,815
for Taegu, and were stopped at Dabu-dong.

28
00:01:09,984 --> 00:01:10,806
So in September,

29
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:12,399
they turned their eyes east.

30
00:01:12,806 --> 00:01:13,781
The attack fell to the

31
00:01:13,801 --> 00:01:16,011
North Korean 15th Division.

32
00:01:16,179 --> 00:01:18,152
It came down through Uiseong and

33
00:01:18,172 --> 00:01:20,155
over the ridges of Mount Bohyeon,

34
00:01:20,175 --> 00:01:22,300
heading south for Yeongcheon.

35
00:01:22,698 --> 00:01:24,681
North of Yeongcheon lies rugged

36
00:01:24,701 --> 00:01:26,639
high ground, Mount Bohyeon at

37
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:30,361
1,124 meters, and Mount Giryong.

38
00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:32,756
Come down off those mountains, and a wide

39
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:35,635
basin opens up along the Geumho River.

40
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:38,311
The town of Yeongcheon sat in the middle

41
00:01:38,331 --> 00:01:41,372
of that basin, on the banks of the Geumho.

42
00:01:41,519 --> 00:01:43,651
For an enemy that had crossed the mountains,

43
00:01:43,671 --> 00:01:45,965
no terrain was left to stop him.

44
00:01:46,354 --> 00:01:47,740
Holding the line here was

45
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,993
the ROK 8th Division.

46
00:01:50,132 --> 00:01:52,480
Worn out, it had fought its way south

47
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,166
through the central mountains since day one.

48
00:01:55,561 --> 00:01:57,411
The North Korean 15th Division

49
00:01:57,431 --> 00:01:59,390
attacked behind its tanks.

50
00:01:59,514 --> 00:02:01,190
The South Korean division did not

51
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:03,610
have a single tank of its own.

52
00:02:03,998 --> 00:02:05,833
Meanwhile, the North Koreans were

53
00:02:05,853 --> 00:02:08,871
attacking along the entire Naktong front.

54
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:13,433
Masan, Waegwan, Dabu-dong, Pohang.

55
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:16,629
Nowhere could the defenders spare a single man.

56
00:02:17,029 --> 00:02:18,458
On the night of September 2nd,

57
00:02:18,478 --> 00:02:20,540
the North Korean attack began.

58
00:02:20,636 --> 00:02:23,211
By dawn the next day, the positions of

59
00:02:23,231 --> 00:02:26,638
the ROK 16th Regiment had collapsed.

60
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:28,649
The 8th Division fell back to the

61
00:02:28,669 --> 00:02:31,688
Giryong ridge and dug a new line.

62
00:02:31,811 --> 00:02:34,022
But before dawn on September 5th,

63
00:02:34,042 --> 00:02:36,936
the enemy struck from three directions at once.

64
00:02:37,339 --> 00:02:39,672
The tanks led the way through the rain.

65
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:41,169
When an enemy tank swung in

66
00:02:41,189 --> 00:02:43,685
behind the line, an entire ROK

67
00:02:43,705 --> 00:02:45,792
regiment scattered in moments.

68
00:02:46,182 --> 00:02:48,632
Korean officers and American advisors

69
00:02:48,652 --> 00:02:50,578
rounded up the scattered men.

70
00:02:50,804 --> 00:02:52,912
Regrouped, the 8th Division turned

71
00:02:52,932 --> 00:02:54,854
to face Yeongcheon once more.

72
00:02:55,255 --> 00:02:57,556
On September 6th, the North Koreans

73
00:02:57,576 --> 00:02:59,843
pushed into the town of Yeongcheon.

74
00:02:59,894 --> 00:03:02,481
The road linking Taegu and Pohang

75
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:05,114
had fallen into enemy hands.

76
00:03:05,497 --> 00:03:07,571
South of town the enemy set a roadblock

77
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:09,608
and cut the road to Gyeongju.

78
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:12,927
By Korean records, the road between Yeongcheon

79
00:03:12,947 --> 00:03:16,334
and Gyeongju stayed closed for five days.

80
00:03:16,727 --> 00:03:19,200
Then ROK Army headquarters and the U.S.

81
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,635
Eighth Army made an urgent call.

82
00:03:21,664 --> 00:03:23,488
Pull regiments out of other sectors,

83
00:03:23,508 --> 00:03:25,413
and send them to Yeongcheon.

84
00:03:25,818 --> 00:03:27,738
The 1st Division's 11th Regiment,

85
00:03:27,758 --> 00:03:31,721
the 6th's 19th, and the new 7th Division's 5th.

86
00:03:31,866 --> 00:03:33,382
Regiments from across the front

87
00:03:33,402 --> 00:03:36,068
raced to Yeongcheon by truck.

88
00:03:36,469 --> 00:03:38,429
Command went to the ROK II Corps

89
00:03:38,449 --> 00:03:41,001
commander, General Yu Jae-hung.

90
00:03:41,141 --> 00:03:43,476
A platoon of American tanks came too,

91
00:03:43,496 --> 00:03:44,284
but the heart of the

92
00:03:44,304 --> 00:03:46,619
fight was South Korean infantry.

93
00:03:47,015 --> 00:03:48,980
From the afternoon of September 6th,

94
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,995
the town changed hands in a seesaw fight.

95
00:03:52,165 --> 00:03:54,580
Each time the ROK troops retook it,

96
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,114
fresh enemy units pushed back in.

97
00:03:57,501 --> 00:04:00,183
On September 7th, the 19th Regiment

98
00:04:00,203 --> 00:04:02,369
ambushed an enemy supply column,

99
00:04:02,389 --> 00:04:04,246
wrecking some thirty trucks.

100
00:04:04,356 --> 00:04:06,385
The enemy's forward infantry was being

101
00:04:06,405 --> 00:04:08,972
cut off from the supplies behind it.

102
00:04:09,357 --> 00:04:12,762
Then, at 2:45 p.m. on September 8th,

103
00:04:12,932 --> 00:04:14,653
South Korean troops finally took

104
00:04:14,673 --> 00:04:16,963
the town of Yeongcheon back.

105
00:04:17,341 --> 00:04:19,908
Now the hunter and the hunted traded places.

106
00:04:20,013 --> 00:04:22,566
The enemy deep in Yeongcheon was now

107
00:04:22,586 --> 00:04:25,062
trapped among South Korean forces.

108
00:04:25,458 --> 00:04:27,787
The 15th Division's artillery had run ahead

109
00:04:27,807 --> 00:04:30,735
of its infantry, and was out of shells.

110
00:04:30,823 --> 00:04:33,353
Korean records say on September 9th,

111
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:36,471
the ROK 5th Regiment overran an enemy

112
00:04:36,491 --> 00:04:38,977
artillery regiment hiding in a tunnel.

113
00:04:39,352 --> 00:04:42,056
On September 10th, ROK II Corps

114
00:04:42,076 --> 00:04:44,457
launched its general counterattack.

115
00:04:44,567 --> 00:04:47,460
It reopened the Gyeongju road and closed

116
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:49,980
in from the south, west and north.

117
00:04:50,384 --> 00:04:52,826
The 21st and 19th Regiments blocked

118
00:04:52,846 --> 00:04:55,033
the paths back into the mountains.

119
00:04:55,156 --> 00:04:56,638
The enemy that had come down to

120
00:04:56,658 --> 00:04:59,712
Yeongcheon now had no way home.

121
00:05:00,116 --> 00:05:01,914
Prisoner interrogations revealed the

122
00:05:01,934 --> 00:05:04,701
location of the enemy division headquarters.

123
00:05:04,756 --> 00:05:07,491
On September 11th, ROK troops struck

124
00:05:07,511 --> 00:05:10,721
it at Daeui-dong, north of Yeongcheon.

125
00:05:11,123 --> 00:05:13,209
South Korean shells rained down on the

126
00:05:13,229 --> 00:05:15,712
headquarters hidden in an apple orchard.

127
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,437
U.S. records say the division's chief of staff,

128
00:05:18,457 --> 00:05:21,235
Colonel Kim Yon, was killed around then.

129
00:05:21,610 --> 00:05:24,601
Its commander, Pak Song-chol, had reportedly

130
00:05:24,621 --> 00:05:27,116
already been relieved for the delay.

131
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:29,891
Leaderless, the enemy abandoned tanks

132
00:05:29,911 --> 00:05:32,348
and guns and scattered into the hills.

133
00:05:32,438 --> 00:05:34,818
That same Pak Song-chol would later

134
00:05:34,838 --> 00:05:37,103
become premier of North Korea.

135
00:05:37,493 --> 00:05:39,414
On September 12th, the 8th

136
00:05:39,434 --> 00:05:41,724
Division restored its broken line.

137
00:05:41,745 --> 00:05:43,285
And by September 13th,

138
00:05:43,305 --> 00:05:45,784
the threat to Yeongcheon was gone.

139
00:05:46,194 --> 00:05:49,470
By Korean records, 3,799 North

140
00:05:49,490 --> 00:05:52,861
Koreans killed and 309 captured.

141
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:55,878
Five tanks destroyed, and 85 vehicles

142
00:05:55,898 --> 00:05:59,546
and 2,327 small arms captured.

143
00:05:59,938 --> 00:06:02,009
The North Korean 15th Division had

144
00:06:02,029 --> 00:06:04,706
effectively ceased to exist as a division.

145
00:06:04,816 --> 00:06:07,106
A rare victory: a South Korean-led

146
00:06:07,126 --> 00:06:10,344
counterattack broke an entire enemy division.

147
00:06:10,741 --> 00:06:12,534
How could a South Korean force without

148
00:06:12,554 --> 00:06:15,242
tanks destroy an entire enemy division?

149
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,729
Let's break it down into four factors.

150
00:06:18,122 --> 00:06:20,744
First, rapid redeployment.

151
00:06:21,134 --> 00:06:22,901
Regiments were pulled from across

152
00:06:22,921 --> 00:06:24,153
the front and massed at

153
00:06:24,173 --> 00:06:26,258
Yeongcheon within a day or two.

154
00:06:26,337 --> 00:06:28,502
The very afternoon the enemy entered

155
00:06:28,522 --> 00:06:31,700
town, they were already arriving.

156
00:06:31,776 --> 00:06:33,850
The U.S. Army's official history called

157
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:36,252
it a notable command achievement.

158
00:06:36,627 --> 00:06:39,263
Second, the collapse of enemy supply.

159
00:06:39,657 --> 00:06:41,010
The enemy had crossed rugged

160
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:43,672
mountains and gone too far; shells

161
00:06:43,692 --> 00:06:45,595
and food could not keep up.

162
00:06:45,650 --> 00:06:47,315
Even after taking the town, he had

163
00:06:47,335 --> 00:06:49,626
no strength left for the next step.

164
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,421
Third, counter-encirclement.

165
00:06:52,813 --> 00:06:55,609
The ROK troops didn't just plug the gap; they

166
00:06:55,629 --> 00:06:58,375
swung behind the enemy and cut his escape.

167
00:06:58,395 --> 00:07:01,356
A battle to hold became a battle to trap.

168
00:07:01,757 --> 00:07:04,092
Fourth, the artillery strike.

169
00:07:04,493 --> 00:07:06,157
South Korean artillery first

170
00:07:06,177 --> 00:07:08,206
silenced the enemy's guns,

171
00:07:08,226 --> 00:07:09,618
then hit the orchard where the

172
00:07:09,638 --> 00:07:11,392
enemy headquarters was hiding.

173
00:07:11,797 --> 00:07:13,967
Two days after the battle, on September

174
00:07:13,987 --> 00:07:17,552
15th, UN forces landed at Inchon.

175
00:07:17,677 --> 00:07:19,592
Because the Naktong line held,

176
00:07:19,612 --> 00:07:22,247
the door to the counteroffensive swung open.

177
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:25,234
On September 16th, the forces on the

178
00:07:25,254 --> 00:07:28,057
Naktong front broke out as well.

179
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:30,167
After nearly three months of retreat,

180
00:07:30,187 --> 00:07:33,301
the tide of the war turned for the first time.

181
00:07:33,703 --> 00:07:35,633
Today, a battle monument and a

182
00:07:35,653 --> 00:07:38,500
memorial park stand in Yeongcheon.

183
00:07:38,567 --> 00:07:40,812
And nearby, at the Yeongcheon National

184
00:07:40,832 --> 00:07:44,152
Cemetery, countless veterans lie at rest.

185
00:07:44,693 --> 00:07:45,982
More than seventy years

186
00:07:46,002 --> 00:07:47,722
have passed since then.

187
00:07:47,859 --> 00:07:49,444
Few remember Yeongcheon the way

188
00:07:49,464 --> 00:07:52,333
they remember Dabu-dong or Inchon.

189
00:07:52,421 --> 00:07:55,058
Yet without those nine days, the September

190
00:07:55,078 --> 00:07:57,656
counteroffensive might never have come.

191
00:07:58,193 --> 00:08:00,484
Nine days that held the waist of the

192
00:08:00,504 --> 00:08:04,091
Naktong line, at its most dangerous moment.

193
00:08:04,187 --> 00:08:05,317
Yeongcheon was where the

194
00:08:05,337 --> 00:08:07,380
great counteroffensive began.

195
00:08:07,783 --> 00:08:09,717
If this story meant something to you,

196
00:08:09,737 --> 00:08:12,449
please subscribe and like the video.

197
00:08:12,519 --> 00:08:13,614
We'll continue to bring the

198
00:08:13,634 --> 00:08:15,915
forgotten records of war to light.
